<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">b. 216 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mani was a prophet born in 216 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, near the Persian capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, who from about 240 preached the religion later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a>. He taught that the world is a mixture of light and darkness and that souls, sparks of light, can be freed through knowledge and discipline, and he presented himself as the last in a line of prophets after the Buddha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroaster">Zoroaster</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. He died in prison under the Sasanian king Bahram I, around 274 to 277, and his faith spread from Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. He described <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a> as one of the four great powers of the world, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and China.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the third century the Persian prophet <b>Mani</b> reportedly counted Aksum alongside Rome, Persia and China as one of the four great kingdoms of the world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A temple at Cao’an near Quanzhou in southern China still holds a stone image of <b>Mani</b> as the “Buddha of Light”, carved in 1339: the only Manichaean shrine in the world known to survive.</blockquote><a href="/religion/manichaeism">Manichaeism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mandaeans, a Gnostic community of southern Iraq and Iran, wrote their scriptures, such as the Ginza Rabba, in Mandaic Aramaic, and the prophet <b>Mani</b> wrote his scriptures in a form of Syriac.</blockquote><a href="/language/aramaic">Aramaic · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>The Manichaean script, which the prophet <b>Mani</b> (216–274) made from his native Aramaic script, was regular and unambiguous, and it shows clearly how the language sounded in the 3rd century.</blockquote><a href="/language/middle-persian">Middle Persian · Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</a></li><li><blockquote>Others followed Manichaeism, the religion of the Persian prophet <b>Mani</b>, and Sogdian Manichaeans converted the Uyghur ruler Bögü Khagan in 762 or 763, making theirs the state religion of the Uyghur Khaganate.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Manichaeism, founded by the prophet <b>Mani</b> in Sasanian Iran in the third century, spread east along the trade routes, and in the 760s the Uyghur ruler Bögü Qaghan adopted it, making it for a time the faith of a steppe empire.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
